1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,564
"Our secrets are safe. They
are masked in the logs."

2
00:00:07,904 --> 00:00:11,814
"We do not store cloud keys
any more. We use OIDC."

3
00:00:12,154 --> 00:00:16,004
"Our release job has contents:
read. It cannot be tampered with."

4
00:00:16,344 --> 00:00:21,341
Teams say each of these in good faith,
and the incidents of this part show where

5
00:00:21,381 --> 00:00:26,314
each belief ends. Masking edits log lines.
It does nothing about a process that

6
00:00:26,354 --> 00:00:30,092
sends the value somewhere else.
OIDC removes the stored key.

7
00:00:30,132 --> 00:00:35,191
Any code in the job can still ask for the
token. And a read-only job token didn't

8
00:00:35,231 --> 00:00:39,976
stop a cache from being written, because
cache writes didn't use that token.

9
00:00:40,316 --> 00:00:45,228
None of the three statements is false. Each
is true about a narrower thing than the

10
00:00:45,268 --> 00:00:48,808
speaker thinks. This video draws
the real boundary for each.

11
00:00:48,848 --> 00:00:52,030
Keep the third one in mind,
the read-only release job.

12
00:00:52,070 --> 00:00:55,132
Its answer is the least
expected, and it comes back.

13
00:00:56,972 --> 00:01:00,515
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:00,555 --> 00:01:05,097
You've now seen four of the five parts
of the model, and the fifth in the last

15
00:01:05,137 --> 00:01:09,737
video. This video is about what a job
holds and what it takes in: the things an

16
00:01:09,777 --> 00:01:12,733
attacker wants, and the
less obvious roads to them.

17
00:01:13,073 --> 00:01:18,203
Four words first. A job is a list of steps
that run on one machine, the runner.

18
00:01:18,243 --> 00:01:22,064
A secret is an encrypted value
stored in GitHub's settings.

19
00:01:22,104 --> 00:01:26,907
Masking means that GitHub replaces a
secret's value with stars in the log.

20
00:01:26,947 --> 00:01:31,750
And OIDC is a way for a job to prove
what it is, in place of a stored key.

21
00:01:32,090 --> 00:01:36,020
Five topics, in the order of
sections 21A.9 to 21A.13.

22
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:41,976
Secrets: scope, and why masking isn't a
boundary. OIDC federation: what id-token:

23
00:01:42,016 --> 00:01:45,725
write grants and where the
access decision is made.

24
00:01:45,765 --> 00:01:51,534
Caches and artifacts as untrusted input.
Self-hosted runners, from the security

25
00:01:51,574 --> 00:01:57,637
side. And environment protection, which you
configured in video 149 and now read as

26
00:01:57,677 --> 00:01:59,034
a security control.

27
00:01:59,374 --> 00:02:04,123
The demonstration removes two controls from
the course's secure workflow and shows

28
00:02:04,163 --> 00:02:08,913
which check notices. It's grep and sed on
a file in the sandbox: one command finds

29
00:02:08,953 --> 00:02:11,950
lines, the other edits them.
Nothing runs on GitHub.

30
00:02:13,790 --> 00:02:15,368
Here's what you'll walk away with.

31
00:02:15,708 --> 00:02:17,121
After this video you can:

32
00:02:17,461 --> 00:02:21,937
say who can read a repository, environment
and organization secret and when;

33
00:02:22,277 --> 00:02:25,460
explain why log masking
does not protect a secret;

34
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:30,071
explain what id-token: write grants
and where the access decision is made;

35
00:02:30,411 --> 00:02:35,070
explain how a cache or an artifact
written by a less trusted run can reach a

36
00:02:35,110 --> 00:02:35,997
privileged one;

37
00:02:36,337 --> 00:02:40,309
state why self-hosted runners and public
repositories are a dangerous combination.

38
00:02:42,149 --> 00:02:47,561
Secrets. In one sentence: a secret is as
exposed as the least trustworthy code in

39
00:02:47,601 --> 00:02:49,244
any job that receives it.

40
00:02:49,584 --> 00:02:54,342
Precisely. Secrets exist at organization,
repository and environment level.

41
00:02:54,382 --> 00:02:59,652
And GitHub Actions can only read a secret
if you explicitly include the secret in a

42
00:02:59,692 --> 00:03:02,786
workflow. Where you include
it sets the exposure.

43
00:03:13,826 --> 00:03:19,142
In the first form the secret is in
workflow-level env, the environment variables

44
00:03:19,182 --> 00:03:24,431
that every step receives. The lint job
has it. The test job has it, and so does

45
00:03:24,471 --> 00:03:28,045
every package whose install
script or test runs there.

46
00:03:28,085 --> 00:03:32,932
In the second form, one step of one
job has it, and that job is behind an

47
00:03:32,972 --> 00:03:38,288
environment, a named gate the job must
pass first. You saw the first form in the

48
00:03:38,328 --> 00:03:43,376
inventory of video 156: a secret named
on line 19 of a file, before any job.

49
00:03:43,716 --> 00:03:45,734
Two facts are routinely misunderstood.

50
00:03:46,074 --> 00:03:50,825
Write access is secret access. The
documentation: "Any user with write access to

51
00:03:50,865 --> 00:03:55,497
your repository has read access to all
secrets configured in your repository."

52
00:03:55,537 --> 00:03:59,450
The reason: they can push a branch
with a workflow that uses them.

53
00:03:59,490 --> 00:04:03,582
The textbook says two of the case
studies are this sentence at scale.

54
00:04:03,622 --> 00:04:08,493
And it names the exception: environment
secrets behind required reviewers make the

55
00:04:08,533 --> 00:04:11,487
sentence false for your
most valuable credentials.

56
00:04:11,827 --> 00:04:16,360
Masking is a courtesy for logs. Registered
secrets are redacted from logs.

57
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:21,426
But the documentation says: "because there
are multiple ways a secret value can be

58
00:04:21,466 --> 00:04:26,554
transformed, this redaction is not guaranteed".
It adds: "never use structured data

59
00:04:26,594 --> 00:04:31,374
as a secret". And: "if an unredacted
secret is sent to a workflow run log, you

60
00:04:31,414 --> 00:04:34,154
should delete the log
and rotate the secret".

61
00:04:34,494 --> 00:04:39,512
Redaction matches known strings in log
output. It does nothing about a process that

62
00:04:39,552 --> 00:04:44,447
sends the value elsewhere, and nothing
about code that reads the runner's memory,

63
00:04:44,487 --> 00:04:48,225
which the Phase 0 report finds
recurring across the incidents.

64
00:04:48,265 --> 00:04:53,161
The textbook's conclusion is the sentence
to remember: the boundary is which code

65
00:04:53,201 --> 00:04:56,086
runs in the job and which
secrets the job holds.

66
00:04:56,426 --> 00:05:01,609
OIDC federation. In one sentence: instead
of storing a cloud key as a secret, the

67
00:05:01,649 --> 00:05:06,897
job asks GitHub for a signed statement of
what it is, and the cloud exchanges that

68
00:05:06,937 --> 00:05:10,186
statement for a credential
valid for that job only.

69
00:05:10,526 --> 00:05:15,702
Precisely. The job or workflow must grant
the id-token: write permission to allow

70
00:05:15,742 --> 00:05:20,532
GitHub's OIDC provider to create a JSON
Web Token, a small signed document.

71
00:05:20,572 --> 00:05:25,491
And the documentation is exact about
what the permission is: it "only enables

72
00:05:25,531 --> 00:05:30,385
fetching and setting the OIDC token; it
does not grant write access to other

73
00:05:30,425 --> 00:05:35,601
resources". The cloud returns a short-lived
access token that is only valid for a

74
00:05:35,641 --> 00:05:36,309
single job.

75
00:05:36,649 --> 00:05:41,506
The token's claims describe the run. A
claim is one stated fact inside the token.

76
00:05:41,546 --> 00:05:46,584
Besides the standard ones there are the
repository and its ID, the owner and its ID,

77
00:05:46,624 --> 00:05:51,601
the visibility, the ref and its type, the
environment, the event name, the workflow

78
00:05:51,641 --> 00:05:55,047
reference, the runner environment,
the actor, and others.

79
00:05:55,087 --> 00:06:00,004
The cloud's trust policy matches on them,
above all on the subject, the claim that

80
00:06:00,044 --> 00:06:03,994
says which repository, and which
branch or environment, is asking.

81
00:06:04,334 --> 00:06:09,139
For a job that references an environment,
the default subject is the repository

82
00:06:09,179 --> 00:06:12,266
followed by environment and
the environment's name.

83
00:06:12,306 --> 00:06:17,050
For a pull request event with no environment:
the repository and pull_request.

84
00:06:17,090 --> 00:06:20,300
For a branch: the repository
and the full branch ref.

85
00:06:20,340 --> 00:06:23,182
For a tag: the repository
and the full tag ref.

86
00:06:23,522 --> 00:06:28,700
Quick quiz. Where is the access decision
made? A, at GitHub, when it signs the

87
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:32,513
token. B, at the cloud provider,
when it reads the token.

88
00:06:32,553 --> 00:06:33,315
Your answer?

89
00:06:38,155 --> 00:06:43,009
B. Not at GitHub. GitHub states facts.
The cloud provider decides, by comparing

90
00:06:43,049 --> 00:06:46,850
those facts with its trust policy,
its own list of conditions.

91
00:06:46,890 --> 00:06:51,682
The OIDC reference: "You must define at
least one condition, so that untrusted

92
00:06:51,722 --> 00:06:55,833
repositories can't request access
tokens for your cloud resources."

93
00:06:55,873 --> 00:06:59,488
The textbook: the trust policy
is where the security lives.

94
00:06:59,828 --> 00:07:04,927
GitHub's guide for one cloud gives a
condition that requires the audience and the

95
00:07:04,967 --> 00:07:08,162
exact subject of one
environment in one repository.

96
00:07:08,202 --> 00:07:13,047
The same guide also shows a wildcard
form: the repository followed by a star.

97
00:07:13,087 --> 00:07:18,313
The textbook tells you how to read that
one: every branch, every tag and every pull

98
00:07:18,353 --> 00:07:21,802
request workflow of the
repository may assume the role.

99
00:07:21,842 --> 00:07:23,198
Prefer the exact form.

100
00:07:23,538 --> 00:07:28,656
Older behavior: the subject contains only
names, so a deleted and re-registered

101
00:07:28,696 --> 00:07:32,574
organization or repository name
could mint the same subject.

102
00:07:32,614 --> 00:07:37,668
Current behavior: repositories created
after the fifteenth of July 2026 use an

103
00:07:37,708 --> 00:07:43,087
immutable default subject format that
includes both the owner ID and the repository

104
00:07:43,127 --> 00:07:43,283
ID.

105
00:07:43,623 --> 00:07:48,984
Renames and transfers after that date switch
as well, and existing repositories can

106
00:07:49,024 --> 00:07:54,451
opt in. Since: opt-in on the twenty-third
of April 2026, default on the fifteenth of

107
00:07:54,491 --> 00:07:59,917
July 2026. Recommended: check which format
your repository issues before writing the

108
00:07:59,957 --> 00:08:04,473
trust policy. A policy in the old
format won't match a new repository.

109
00:08:04,813 --> 00:08:09,633
Subject customization lets an organization
or repository change which claims form

110
00:08:09,673 --> 00:08:14,613
the subject, for example to require the
reusable workflow reference, so that only a

111
00:08:14,653 --> 00:08:17,553
particular reusable workflow
can obtain the role.

112
00:08:17,893 --> 00:08:21,248
What OIDC does not do. It
removes the long-lived key.

113
00:08:21,288 --> 00:08:26,371
It doesn't make the job trustworthy. The
textbook quotes a post-mortem, a team's

114
00:08:26,411 --> 00:08:31,046
published account of its incident:
"OIDC trusted-publisher binding has no

115
00:08:31,086 --> 00:08:35,978
per-publish review. Once configured, any
code path in the workflow can mint a

116
00:08:36,018 --> 00:08:39,308
publish-capable token." So
the rule has three parts.

117
00:08:39,348 --> 00:08:44,688
Grant id-token: write to one job. Bind the
subject to an environment with protection

118
00:08:44,728 --> 00:08:48,274
rules. And let no untrusted
code or cache into that job.

119
00:08:48,614 --> 00:08:53,575
Caches and artifacts. In one sentence: a
cache is a shared, unsigned directory keyed

120
00:08:53,615 --> 00:08:58,277
by a string. Whoever can write an entry
under the key your release job computes

121
00:08:58,317 --> 00:09:00,182
controls what that job restores.

122
00:09:00,522 --> 00:09:05,404
Precisely. The documentation: "Anyone with
read access can create a pull request on

123
00:09:05,444 --> 00:09:10,385
a repository and access the contents of a
cache", and "cache contents are not signed

124
00:09:10,425 --> 00:09:15,070
or verified". A run can restore caches
from its own branch and from the default

125
00:09:15,110 --> 00:09:15,485
branch.

126
00:09:15,825 --> 00:09:20,653
A pull_request run saves to its own merge
ref, which other branches can't restore.

127
00:09:20,693 --> 00:09:25,640
A privileged trigger runs in the default
branch's context, and that was the opening.

128
00:09:25,680 --> 00:09:30,628
The post-mortem records: "cache writes use
a runner-internal token, not the workflow

129
00:09:30,668 --> 00:09:35,437
GITHUB_TOKEN… Setting permissions: contents:
read does not block cache mutation."

130
00:09:35,777 --> 00:09:40,310
That's the third sentence of the hook,
answered. The read-only job token was never

131
00:09:40,350 --> 00:09:43,823
the token that writes the cache.
Two platform changes followed.

132
00:09:44,163 --> 00:09:49,589
The twenty-sixth of June 2026: only push,
workflow_dispatch, repository_dispatch,

133
00:09:49,629 --> 00:09:55,190
delete, registry_package, page_build and
schedule can create or overwrite caches in

134
00:09:55,230 --> 00:10:00,792
the default branch's scope. Other triggers
that resolve to it get read-only access.

135
00:10:01,132 --> 00:10:06,366
The tenth of September 2026: cache-mode,
at workflow or job level, with the values

136
00:10:06,406 --> 00:10:11,319
read, write, write-only and none. The
job value overrides the workflow value.

137
00:10:11,359 --> 00:10:16,401
And a warning from the changelog: explicitly
declaring write or write-only on a

138
00:10:16,441 --> 00:10:20,196
low-trust trigger reintroduces
the risk of cache poisoning.

139
00:10:20,536 --> 00:10:25,692
Setup actions cache implicitly, so check
them too. And artifacts, the files one job

140
00:10:25,732 --> 00:10:29,072
uploads for another to
download, follow the same rule.

141
00:10:29,112 --> 00:10:34,330
The documentation: "A workflow_run workflow
should treat artifacts uploaded by other

142
00:10:34,370 --> 00:10:38,838
workflows as untrusted data, since
their contents can come from a fork."

143
00:10:39,178 --> 00:10:44,102
Self-hosted runners. In one sentence: a
self-hosted runner is your machine, on your

144
00:10:44,142 --> 00:10:49,006
network, executing whatever the workflow
says, and it keeps its state between jobs

145
00:10:49,046 --> 00:10:50,740
unless you make it ephemeral.

146
00:10:51,080 --> 00:10:55,481
The documentation: "Self-hosted runners
should almost never be used for public

147
00:10:55,521 --> 00:10:59,751
repositories on GitHub, because any
user can open pull requests against the

148
00:10:59,791 --> 00:11:04,249
repository and compromise the environment."
Private repositories aren't exempt:

149
00:11:04,289 --> 00:11:08,804
anyone who can fork the repository and
open a pull request, generally those with

150
00:11:08,844 --> 00:11:13,131
read access, can do the same. GitHub-hosted
runners are "ephemeral and clean

151
00:11:13,171 --> 00:11:17,686
isolated virtual machines". Self-hosted
ones "can be persistently compromised by

152
00:11:17,726 --> 00:11:19,394
untrusted code in a workflow".

153
00:11:19,734 --> 00:11:23,664
The fork approval gate doesn't help
once a contributor is past it.

154
00:11:23,704 --> 00:11:27,453
And on self-hosted runners,
environments don't isolate secrets.

155
00:11:27,793 --> 00:11:32,389
The documented controls. Ephemeral runners
that take one job and are removed.

156
00:11:32,429 --> 00:11:36,243
Runner groups that restrict which
repositories may use a runner.

157
00:11:36,283 --> 00:11:40,879
The organization setting that restricts
or disables repository-level runners.

158
00:11:40,919 --> 00:11:43,468
And approval for all
external contributors.

159
00:11:43,808 --> 00:11:48,591
Environment protection. In one sentence:
an environment puts a human decision, a

160
00:11:48,631 --> 00:11:53,354
branch condition, or both between a job
and the secrets and cloud identity that

161
00:11:53,394 --> 00:11:55,162
belong to a deployment target.

162
00:11:55,502 --> 00:12:00,624
You know the mechanics from video 149.
For security review three details matter.

163
00:12:00,664 --> 00:12:06,043
Referencing a name that doesn't exist creates
an environment with no rules: a typing

164
00:12:06,083 --> 00:12:11,333
error in the name silently removes the
gate. Deployment branch rules are evaluated

165
00:12:11,373 --> 00:12:16,624
against the ref the run executes on: since
the eighth of December 2025 that is the

166
00:12:16,664 --> 00:12:21,849
merge ref for pull_request events and the
default branch for pull_request_target.

167
00:12:22,189 --> 00:12:27,192
And required reviewers and wait timers are
plan-gated, meaning they depend on your

168
00:12:27,232 --> 00:12:31,805
GitHub plan: on Free, Pro and Team they
exist only for public repositories.

169
00:12:32,145 --> 00:12:37,406
And the combination that makes the whole
design hold: with OIDC, an environment name

170
00:12:37,446 --> 00:12:42,519
in the subject means the cloud role can
be assumed only by a job that passed that

171
00:12:42,559 --> 00:12:43,781
environment's rules.

172
00:12:45,621 --> 00:12:50,781
Analogy for OIDC, from the textbook. A
notarized letter of introduction in place of

173
00:12:50,821 --> 00:12:55,729
a copied house key. The cloud reads the
letter, which names the repository, the

174
00:12:55,769 --> 00:12:59,238
branch and the environment,
and decides whether to open.

175
00:12:59,578 --> 00:13:04,066
The analogy breaks at one point: any code
running in the job can ask the notary for

176
00:13:04,106 --> 00:13:04,666
the letter.

177
00:13:05,006 --> 00:13:09,627
Take that sentence seriously and the
three topics of this video become one.

178
00:13:09,667 --> 00:13:14,660
A secret in the environment, an OIDC token
on request, the job token: all of them

179
00:13:14,700 --> 00:13:18,264
are available to every piece
of code that runs in the job.

180
00:13:18,304 --> 00:13:22,179
Masking doesn't change that. Short
lifetimes don't change that.

181
00:13:22,219 --> 00:13:25,908
A read-only job token doesn't
change that for the other two.

182
00:13:26,248 --> 00:13:31,497
So there are only two real levers. What the
job holds: as little as possible, in as

183
00:13:31,537 --> 00:13:36,467
few jobs as possible, behind a gate. And
what code and input reach the job: no

184
00:13:36,507 --> 00:13:41,565
outsider's code, no cache it didn't build,
no artifact it treats as anything but

185
00:13:41,605 --> 00:13:41,884
data.

186
00:13:42,224 --> 00:13:47,631
That's the question from video 156 again,
now with a full answer to its third part:

187
00:13:47,671 --> 00:13:51,503
"what can the job reach" includes
what the job can ask for.

188
00:13:53,343 --> 00:13:58,438
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. Draw
a job as a box with three steps: your

189
00:13:58,478 --> 00:14:02,547
script, a third-party action, and
a dependency's install script.

190
00:14:02,587 --> 00:14:07,554
The job holds one secret in env. Mark
every step that can read it, and say the

191
00:14:07,594 --> 00:14:08,581
number out loud.

192
00:14:13,421 --> 00:14:18,180
All three. In the first picture, the mask
and the leak are unrelated: the log shows

193
00:14:18,220 --> 00:14:20,781
stars, and the value
left through step three.

194
00:14:21,121 --> 00:14:26,144
In the second picture, the OIDC exchange,
the only place where "no" can be said is

195
00:14:26,184 --> 00:14:31,145
the trust policy. If its condition is a
wildcard, it says yes to every branch and

196
00:14:31,185 --> 00:14:33,985
every pull request workflow
of the repository.

197
00:14:35,825 --> 00:14:37,566
Step 1: the baseline.

198
00:14:37,906 --> 00:14:42,684
Two checks that a team could run in CI,
its automated checks on every change.

199
00:14:42,724 --> 00:14:47,814
The first lists every uses line that isn't
pinned to forty hexadecimal characters.

200
00:14:47,854 --> 00:14:52,319
The second counts the checkouts that
don't leave the credential on disk.

201
00:14:52,359 --> 00:14:56,323
On an intact workflow 12, what
does each print? Say it out loud.

202
00:15:01,163 --> 00:15:06,691
A clean working tree. Six uses lines. The
unpinned scan returns nothing, with exit

203
00:15:06,731 --> 00:15:12,394
status 1 from grep: every action is pinned.
And four lines with persist-credentials:

204
00:15:12,434 --> 00:15:12,801
false.

205
00:15:13,141 --> 00:15:15,989
Step 2: a "cleanup" that many
reviewers would approve.

206
00:15:16,329 --> 00:15:20,863
The comment in the transcript describes
it the way its author would: readable

207
00:15:20,903 --> 00:15:25,674
version tags, less boilerplate. The sed
command edits a file in the working tree.

208
00:15:25,714 --> 00:15:27,397
Git has recorded nothing yet.

209
00:15:27,737 --> 00:15:33,329
Three insertions, seven deletions. Imagine
this diff in a pull request titled "Tidy

210
00:15:33,369 --> 00:15:38,690
the CI file". It is shorter. It is more
readable. Nothing about it looks like a

211
00:15:38,730 --> 00:15:41,337
security change. Two controls are gone.

212
00:15:41,677 --> 00:15:43,871
Step 3: which check notices.

213
00:15:44,211 --> 00:15:50,128
The unpinned scan now prints three lines,
36, 82 and 111, each a checkout by tag,

214
00:15:50,168 --> 00:15:55,938
with exit status 0: found. And the count
of persist-credentials: false is zero.

215
00:15:55,978 --> 00:16:00,644
Both checks noticed. A reviewer
reading the diff might not have.

216
00:16:00,984 --> 00:16:05,812
Now the limit of this demonstration, which
the lab's objective states: you remove

217
00:16:05,852 --> 00:16:10,378
two controls and see which checks notice.
These two can be found by pattern.

218
00:16:10,418 --> 00:16:15,246
Workflow 12 marks nine controls with
comments. Ask yourself which of the others a

219
00:16:15,286 --> 00:16:19,212
grep could detect if removed, and
which only a person would catch.

220
00:16:19,252 --> 00:16:23,899
Removing cache-mode: none from the deploy
job? Removing the environment block?

221
00:16:23,939 --> 00:16:27,684
Changing the if that keeps pull
requests out of the deploy job?

222
00:16:27,724 --> 00:16:29,186
That question is the lab.

223
00:16:29,526 --> 00:16:31,166
Step 4: recover.

224
00:16:31,506 --> 00:16:34,878
git restore on a path is 🔴
DANGEROUS, so the five answers.

225
00:16:34,918 --> 00:16:39,585
What it changes: the file in the working
tree, back to the version in the index.

226
00:16:39,625 --> 00:16:43,997
What it can destroy: uncommitted edits
to that file that were never staged.

227
00:16:44,037 --> 00:16:47,704
How to preview: git diff for the
path, which you saw as a stat.

228
00:16:47,744 --> 00:16:50,940
How to recover: none for
content that was never staged.

229
00:16:50,980 --> 00:16:55,882
When it is appropriate: here, because the
edit was the experiment, and discarding it

230
00:16:55,922 --> 00:16:56,588
is the goal.

231
00:16:56,928 --> 00:17:00,890
Clean status, the scan is empty
again, and the count is back to four.

232
00:17:01,230 --> 00:17:06,225
One picture of what happened. The cleanup
edited the file in the working tree only.

233
00:17:06,265 --> 00:17:11,017
Nothing was staged, and nothing was
committed. So restore had one job: copy the

234
00:17:11,057 --> 00:17:13,444
version in the index back over the edit.

235
00:17:13,784 --> 00:17:18,885
Read the deploy job with this video's
five topics. Its if admits only a push to

236
00:17:18,925 --> 00:17:24,155
main: only trusted code reaches this job.
It names an environment: the protection

237
00:17:24,195 --> 00:17:27,669
rules, and the environment's
name in the OIDC subject.

238
00:17:27,709 --> 00:17:32,680
Its permissions are contents: read and
id-token: write: the only job that may

239
00:17:32,720 --> 00:17:34,112
request an OIDC token.

240
00:17:34,452 --> 00:17:37,929
cache-mode: none: it neither
restores nor saves a cache.

241
00:17:37,969 --> 00:17:43,018
And there is no stored cloud key anywhere
in the file: the role is assumed with a

242
00:17:43,058 --> 00:17:47,101
short-lived token. Search the whole
file for the secrets context.

243
00:17:47,141 --> 00:17:48,169
It doesn't occur.

244
00:17:48,509 --> 00:17:53,350
Now Lab 29.2, Part B, on your practice
repository, in your normal shell.

245
00:17:53,390 --> 00:17:58,978
The interface changes. The lab text and the
linked documentation are the reference.

246
00:17:59,018 --> 00:18:02,029
No GitHub output was
captured by the authors.

247
00:18:02,369 --> 00:18:06,637
Add the workflow through a pull request
and look at the list of checks.

248
00:18:06,677 --> 00:18:11,734
Predict first which jobs run for a pull
request and which are skipped, from their if

249
00:18:11,774 --> 00:18:15,495
conditions. The deploy job is
skipped for two reasons at once.

250
00:18:15,535 --> 00:18:20,410
Name both. Then the lab has you make the
title job fail on purpose by editing the

251
00:18:20,450 --> 00:18:20,774
title.

252
00:18:21,114 --> 00:18:25,790
Notice what the lab tells you: editing the
title doesn't start a new run, because

253
00:18:25,830 --> 00:18:29,807
the trigger without types reacts to
opened, synchronize and reopened.

254
00:18:29,847 --> 00:18:34,580
You push an empty commit to start one, read
the failed job, restore the title, and

255
00:18:34,620 --> 00:18:35,221
push again.

256
00:18:37,061 --> 00:18:38,709
Five mistakes to watch for.

257
00:18:39,049 --> 00:18:43,908
Putting a secret in workflow-level env.
Root cause: every step of every job,

258
00:18:43,948 --> 00:18:49,258
including third-party actions and dependencies'
install scripts, then has it in its

259
00:18:49,298 --> 00:18:50,031
environment.

260
00:18:50,371 --> 00:18:55,403
Treating log masking as protection. Root
cause: redaction matches known strings in

261
00:18:55,443 --> 00:19:00,041
log output; it does nothing about a
process that sends the value elsewhere.

262
00:19:00,381 --> 00:19:05,442
A wildcard subject in the cloud's trust
policy. Root cause: it lets every branch,

263
00:19:05,482 --> 00:19:10,479
every tag and every pull request workflow
of the repository assume the role; the

264
00:19:10,519 --> 00:19:13,628
trust policy is where the
access decision is made.

265
00:19:13,968 --> 00:19:17,191
Letting a job with a publishing
identity restore a cache.

266
00:19:17,231 --> 00:19:21,885
Root cause: cache contents are not signed,
and a less trusted run may have written

267
00:19:21,925 --> 00:19:24,232
the entry under the key the job computes.

268
00:19:24,572 --> 00:19:27,945
Believing permissions: contents:
read protects the cache.

269
00:19:27,985 --> 00:19:32,317
Root cause: cache writes used a
runner-internal token, not the job token.

270
00:19:34,157 --> 00:19:38,944
Now, out of the lab. Picture a team that
publishes an evaluation library to a

271
00:19:38,984 --> 00:19:44,084
package registry. Two years ago it stored
a registry token as a repository secret.

272
00:19:44,124 --> 00:19:49,350
Last year it moved to trusted publishing
with OIDC and deleted the stored token, and

273
00:19:49,390 --> 00:19:54,051
wrote in its security notes that
publishing credentials can no longer leak.

274
00:19:54,391 --> 00:19:59,116
Review that with this video. The stored
key is gone: good, nothing long-lived to

275
00:19:59,156 --> 00:20:04,000
steal. But which job has id-token: write?
If it's set at workflow level, every job

276
00:20:04,040 --> 00:20:08,706
can request the token, including the test
job that runs the dependencies' code.

277
00:20:09,046 --> 00:20:13,678
Is the publishing job behind an environment
with rules, and is the environment's

278
00:20:13,718 --> 00:20:18,409
name in the subject the registry checks?
Does the publishing job restore a cache,

279
00:20:18,449 --> 00:20:23,140
and which runs can write under that key?
Does any step in it run code that didn't

280
00:20:23,180 --> 00:20:25,243
come from a reviewed commit on main?

281
00:20:25,583 --> 00:20:30,323
The textbook's summary of what OIDC
changes fits on one line: it removes the

282
00:20:30,363 --> 00:20:33,845
long-lived key, and it doesn't
make the job trustworthy.

283
00:20:33,885 --> 00:20:39,128
The team's notes should say which job can
ask for the identity, what gates that job,

284
00:20:39,168 --> 00:20:40,889
and what is allowed into it.

285
00:20:42,729 --> 00:20:49,114
Your turn. Do Lab 29.2, "Justify
every control of workflow 12", in

286
00:20:49,154 --> 00:20:55,150
lab-manual/m29-actions-security.md.
Part A runs in labs/shell.

287
00:20:55,490 --> 00:21:00,404
Write the table the lab asks for: control,
line, threat, and what breaks without it.

288
00:21:00,444 --> 00:21:05,063
The file marks nine controls with comments.
Find at least two more that have no

289
00:21:05,103 --> 00:21:09,722
comment. For each control, predict before
you test whether a pattern scan would

290
00:21:09,762 --> 00:21:10,843
notice its removal.

291
00:21:11,183 --> 00:21:16,816
The challenge is Exercise 29.6,
"Who can assume the role?", in

292
00:21:16,856 --> 00:21:20,293
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

293
00:21:22,133 --> 00:21:25,348
Read the question on screen.
Say your answer out loud.

294
00:21:30,188 --> 00:21:34,377
A strong answer is precise about the
permission: what it enables and what it

295
00:21:34,417 --> 00:21:38,939
explicitly doesn't. It then follows the
exchange: what the token states, who reads

296
00:21:38,979 --> 00:21:43,501
it, and what is compared with what. It
names the claim that matters most and gives

297
00:21:43,541 --> 00:21:48,119
the difference between an exact and a
wildcard condition in terms of who can assume

298
00:21:48,159 --> 00:21:48,620
the role.

299
00:21:48,960 --> 00:21:53,662
It ends with what federation doesn't solve.
The follow-up is a trust policy that

300
00:21:53,702 --> 00:21:57,634
matches for older repositories and
not for one created this summer.

301
00:21:57,674 --> 00:22:02,258
You heard the version note, so say what
changed and how you would diagnose it.

302
00:22:04,098 --> 00:22:05,025
Let's land this.

303
00:22:05,365 --> 00:22:07,025
You should now be able to say:

304
00:22:07,365 --> 00:22:12,478
A secret is as exposed as the least
trustworthy code in any job that receives it;

305
00:22:12,518 --> 00:22:17,757
write access to the repository is access
to its secrets, except environment secrets

306
00:22:17,797 --> 00:22:19,411
behind required reviewers.

307
00:22:19,751 --> 00:22:24,236
Masking edits log lines; the boundary
is which code runs in the job and which

308
00:22:24,276 --> 00:22:25,529
secrets the job holds.

309
00:22:25,869 --> 00:22:31,242
id-token: write only lets the job fetch
an OIDC token; the cloud's trust policy

310
00:22:31,282 --> 00:22:34,599
makes the access decision,
mostly on the subject.

311
00:22:34,939 --> 00:22:39,800
Caches are shared and unsigned, artifacts
from other workflows can come from a fork;

312
00:22:39,840 --> 00:22:44,117
a job with an identity uses cache-mode:
none and treats artifacts as data.

313
00:22:44,457 --> 00:22:49,189
A persistent self-hosted runner keeps what
a job left, and an environment puts its

314
00:22:49,229 --> 00:22:50,644
gate in front of the job.

315
00:22:52,484 --> 00:22:57,824
Read sections 21A.9 to
21A.13 of Chapter 21A.

316
00:22:58,164 --> 00:23:03,729
Today you drew the real boundary behind
three comfortable sentences, and watched two

317
00:23:03,769 --> 00:23:09,067
controls vanish in a tidy-looking diff.
Run that demo yourself in the lab shell.

318
00:23:09,107 --> 00:23:14,604
Next time: static analysis of workflows,
the platform changes of 2025 and 2026, and

319
00:23:14,644 --> 00:23:18,941
workflow 12. Until then, look at
the state first and type second.

320
00:23:18,981 --> 00:23:20,543
See you in the next one.
